.g., acanthosis nigricans) neutralizes this evidence for metabolic syndrome (B4)."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the painful rash and lack of abdominal mass or endocrine tumor-specific markers (e.g., elevated cortisol metabolites) argue against Endocrine Tumor with Hormonal Overproduction as the primary me…", "summary": "The presence of a painful rash and absence of tumor markers or abdominal mass moderately weakens endocrine tumor (B1) as the primary explanation."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of typical hypercortisolism features (moon face, striae) and presence of painful rash rule out Hypercortisolism as a life-threatening alternative?", "summary": "Classic hypercortisolism signs are absent, and the painful rash is inconsistent with Cushing syndrome, providing strong evidence against hypercortisolism (B3)."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient’s extreme hyperglycaemia (>500 mg/dL) with concurrent multisystem symptoms (rash, GI) argue against Metabolic Syndrome with Insulin Resistance as the dominant mechanism?", "summary": "Extreme hyperglycaemia with multisystem symptoms moderately reduces the likelihood of metabolic syndrome (B4) as the primary mechanism, though obesity still provides some indirect support."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.421], ["B4", 0.4], ["B3", 0.104]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.739], ["B1.1", 0.329], ["B1.2", 0.246]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Extreme hyperglycaemia (>500 mg/dL) is a medical emergency requiring immediate stabilization before determining underlying pathophysiology.", "required_actions": ["Initiate insulin therapy", "Monitor for hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) signs", "Order electrolytes and serum glucose confirmatory testing"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "[static mode override] Active emergency interrupt: Extreme hyperglycaem