minal pain, abnormality of lipoprotein cholesterol concentration, abnormality of the skin, accelerated atherosclerosis, angina pectoris\n\nMetabolic Syndrome with Insulin Resistance vs Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormal circulating fatty-acid concentration, abnormal circulating lipid concentration, abnormal oral glucose tolerance, abnormal salivary gland morphology, abnormality of body weight\n  Favours second: abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormal blistering of the skin, abnormal gastrointestinal motility, abnormality of the thyroid gland, acanthocytosis\n\nMetabolic Syndrome with Insulin Resistance vs VIPoma (Enteroglucagon Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormal circulating fatty-acid concentration, abnormal circulating lipid concentration, abnormal oral glucose tolerance, abnormal salivary gland morphology, abnormality of body weight\n  Favours second: abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormal gastrointestinal motility, abnormality of the thyroid gland, anorexia, ascites\n\nMetabolic Syndrome with Insulin Resistance vs Pheochromocytoma (Adrenal Medulla Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormal circulating fatty-acid concentration, abnormal circulating lipid concentration, abnormal oral glucose tolerance, abnormal salivary gland morphology, abnormality of body weight\n  Favours second: adrenal pheochromocytoma, cerebral hemorrhage, chest pain, conductive hearing impairment, congestive heart failure\n\nReactive Hyperglycemia vs Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormality of lipoprotein cholesterol concentration, abnormality of the skin, accelerated atherosclerosis, angina pectoris\n  Favours second: abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormal blistering of the skin, abnormal gastrointestinal motility, abnormality of the thyroid gland, acanthocytosis\n\nReactive Hyperglycemia vs VIPoma (Enteroglucagon Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormality of lipoprotein cholesterol concentration, abnormality of the skin, accelerated atherosclerosis, angina pectoris\n  Favours second: abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormal gastrointestinal motility, abnormality of the thyroid gland, anorexia, ascites\n\nReactive Hyperglycemia vs Pheochromocytoma (Adrenal Medulla Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormality of lipoprotein cholesterol concentration, abnormality of the skin, accelerated atherosclerosis, angina pectoris\n  Favours second: adrenal pheochromocytoma, cerebral hemorrhage, chest pain, conductive hearing impairment, congestive heart failure\n\nGlucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) vs VIPoma (Enteroglucagon Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormal blistering of the skin, acanthocytosis, depressivity, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, glossitis\n  Favours second: benign gastrointestinal tract tumors, dehydration, elevated calcitonin, erythema, generalized muscle weakness\n\nGlucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) vs Pheochromocytoma (Adrenal Medulla Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormal blistering of the skin, abnormal gastrointestinal motility, abnormality of the thyroid gland, acanthocytosis\n  Favours second: adrenal pheochromocytoma, cerebral hemorrhage, chest pain, conductive hearing impairment, congestive heart failure\n\nVIPoma (Enteroglucagon Cell Tumor) vs Pheochromocytoma (Adrenal Medulla Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormal gastrointestinal motility, abnormality of the thyroid gland, anorexia, ascites\n  Favours second: adrenal pheochromocytoma, cerebral hemorrhage, chest pain, conductive hearing impairment, congestive heart failure\n\nNOT typically seen in VIPoma (Enteroglucagon Cell Tumor): hematochezia\n\nRelated to Metabolic Syndrome with Insulin Resistance: diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\nRelated to Reactive Hyperglycemia: glucose metabolism disease\n\nRelated to Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor): pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor\n\nRelated to VIPoma (Enteroglucagon Cell Tumor): pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor, pancreatic vasoactive intestinal peptide producing tumor, small intestinal vasoactive intestinal peptide producing tumor\n\nRelated to Pheochromocytoma (Adrenal Medulla Tumor): sporadic pheochromocytoma/secreting paraganglioma\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.62):]\n  Source: Cushing Syndrome: Diagnostic Workup and Imaging Features, With Clinical and Pathologic Correlation. > Introduction"}
